Z||000017||pontiacs4me||10-20-2001||03:45 AM||||I prefer the 3800 series II supercharged myself. I should be able to complete the swap for under $3000 and WILL have 340+ horse at the crank.  See ya 2.8!<P>------------------<BR>Soon to be: L67 powered 87 coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000018||TRiAD||10-20-2001||10:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>I prefer the 3800 series II supercharged myself. I should be able to complete the swap for under $3000 and WILL have 340+ horse at the crank.  See ya 2.8!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>PLEASE let me know how this swap goes for you. The 2.8SC kit is a project I'm going to do BEFORE I swap my engine, and the 3800SC is one of the engines I'm considering. <P>Also, I'll be using a custom intake manifold, with the SC maounted AFTER the throttle body, and on the manifold. It will be for the 2.8 only, but manifolds and brackets can be fabbed for any engine I can get my hands on. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||GTFiero1||10-20-2001||06:50 PM||||340hp for under $3,00 bucks?! you have to know everyon one in the bussiness for that! the cheapest kit i've seen is 1,800 ans those engines run around 2,000 for a used one. they come with 240hp not 340. in order to get it up to 340 you would need another 3,000 dollars- a supercharged mod and intercooler. <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com" TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com</A>   if you can do all this for 3,000 than you should start your own business installing these for people<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000020||DaRkLoRD||10-20-2001||07:17 PM||||you could get that with a modified 3800 SC (ported, polished etc) and a big shot of nos<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000021||pontiacs4me||10-20-2001||11:55 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTFiero1:<BR><B>340hp for under $3,00 bucks?! you have to know everyon one in the bussiness for that! the cheapest kit i've seen is 1,800 ans those engines run around 2,000 for a used one. they come with 240hp not 340. in order to get it up to 340 you would need another 3,000 dollars- a supercharged mod and intercooler. <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com" TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com</A>   if you can do all this for 3,000 than you should start your own business installing these for people<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I know all about these engines, first off I paid $1694 for my engine tranny and wiring.  I spent $348 for bearings rings and gaskets, Im porting the heads, intake and blower, building custom headers, a friend of mine makes what is called the supercooler, which is better than an intercooler, im adding a 3.0" pulley, and a multitude of things we make that I am not allowed to discuss at this time.  <BR>Oh yeah, about an installation kit, I can make my own for about $20, and some inginuity.  My friend that makes the supercooler and myself are starting a business called animuL Performance, specializing in pontiac grand prix GTP aftermarket performance.  Look for me at the oklahoma city fiero fest next summer, hot rod power tour 2002 and the fiero power fest if it goes down.  <BR>My goal is to have the fastest daily driven v6 powered fiero in North America.<BR>My friend Tom (see <A HREF="http://www.luminasupercharged.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.luminasupercharged.com</A>  ) has a L67 in a 91 lumina, the car weighs 700 lbs more than mine and runs 12.747 at 108.25 in the 1/4.  Ill leave the rest to your imagination, but with mine I see mid 11's in my cars future!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by pontiacs4me (edited 10-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||TRiAD||11-13-2001||03:33 PM||||<u><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Update</B></FONT></u><P>I have the GT now, but it need some work to get it on the road (clutch related). <P>I have also decided to use a NEW SC for this kit, and not the one I had. The new one has the bypass BUILT IN, and is far superior to the old one, for about the same price. <P>It will be a little while before I can begin work on this, but I still plan on having it ready for Spring. (before April). <P>Thanks for all the feedback, I'll keep you posted...<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">     <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
